    <node id="protein:O42629">
      <data key="d0">PROTEIN</data>
      <data key="d1">Fructosyl amine-oxygen oxidoreductase I</data>
      <data key="d2">Generation of Amadori products is the major single modification by the Maillard reaction in vivo and a source of biologically active glycoxidation products leading to protein cross-linking in biological tissues. Amadoriases are FAD enzymes which catalyze the oxidative deglycation of Amadori products into deoxyglucosone, hydrogen peroxide, and the corresponding primary amine (PubMed:10684633, PubMed:15629109, PubMed:16037311, PubMed:17439148, PubMed:18667417, PubMed:26873906). The substrate is oxidized by the flavin in the reductive half-reaction, and the reduced flavin is then oxidized by molecular oxygen in the oxidative half-reaction (By similarity). Amadori products formed on free amino acids are a good substrate for amadoriase I, as well as oligopeptides up to four amino acids in length, whereas the enzyme is unable to cleave Amadori products formed on whole proteins (PubMed:16037311)</data>
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">UniProt:O42629</data>
      <data key="d7">Aspergillus fumigatus</data>
      <data key="d20">1.5.3.25</data>
      <data key="d21" />
      <data key="d22">4WCT, 4XWZ</data>
      <data key="d23">445</data>
      <data key="d24">True</data>
    </node>
    <node id="protein:Q4WIF5">
