      <data key="d2">Generation of Amadori products is the major single modification by the Maillard reaction in vivo and a source of biologically active glycoxidation products leading to protein cross-linking in biological tissues. Amadoriases are FAD enzymes which catalyze the oxidative deglycation of Amadori products into deoxyglucosone, hydrogen peroxide, and the corresponding primary amine (PubMed:10684633, PubMed:15629109, PubMed:16037311, PubMed:17439148, PubMed:18667417, PubMed:29445091). The substrate is oxidized by the flavin in the reductive half-reaction, and the reduced flavin is then oxidized by molecular oxygen in the oxidative half-reaction (By similarity). Amadori products formed on free amino acids are a good substrate for amadoriase I, as well as oligopeptides up to four amino acids in length, whereas the enzyme is unable to cleave Amadori products formed on whole proteins (PubMed:16037311)</data>
